---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import NewsletterSignup from '../../components/NewsletterSignup.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Why does ECD matter if the mud weight in the pits hasn\'t changed?',
    a: 'Static mud weight only tells you the pressure of a column sitting still. The moment pumps are on, friction against the annulus walls adds extra pressure on top of that column — ECD is what the formation actually sees while circulating, and it is always higher than static mud weight, never lower. A well that looks comfortably inside the drilling window on paper can still take losses at the shoe the moment circulation starts if that friction margin was never checked.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does this calculation only use annular pressure loss, not the pressure loss inside the drill string?',
    a: 'Pressure loss inside the drill string pushes fluid toward the bit, which shows up as extra pump pressure at surface — it never adds to the pressure column sitting on the formation. Pressure loss in the annulus works the opposite direction: fluid climbing back to surface has to fight friction the whole way up, and that resistance adds directly to the load pressing down on the open hole. Only the annular side counts toward ECD.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does a narrow annulus push ECD up faster than a wide one?',
    a: 'Annular pressure loss in this formula scales with velocity squared and is divided by hole size minus pipe size — shrink that gap and the fluid has to speed up to move the same flow rate through less area, and the friction penalty climbs fast, not in a straight line. A large-OD BHA in a small hole, a full-gauge <a href="/equipment/stabilizer">stabilizer</a>, or a long liner lap, is exactly where ECD margin disappears first, well before TVD or flow rate looks unusual on their own — it is also why an <a href="/equipment/under-reamer">under-reamer</a> opening hole below a restriction can ease ECD in a section that would otherwise run the whole interval at bit gauge.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What counts as a safe ECD margin below fracture gradient?',
    a: 'There is no universal number — it depends on formation strength, how well the fracture gradient at that depth is actually known, and how much lost-circulation risk the operation can tolerate. What matters is checking ECD against the same fracture-gradient line used on the <a href="/calculators/mud-weight-window">mud weight window</a>, not just against static mud weight, since that static comparison alone can hide a real problem once pumps are running.',
  },
];



const webAppLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebApplication",
  "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/ecd#app",
  "name": "ECD Calculator: Equivalent Circulating Density While Drilling",
  "url": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/ecd",
  "description": "Free ECD calculator: annular velocity, annular friction pressure loss, and equivalent circulating density from mud weight, flow rate and annulus geometry.",
  "applicationCategory": "UtilitiesApplication",
  "applicationSubCategory": "Drilling engineering calculator",
  "operatingSystem": "Any (web browser)",
  "browserRequirements": "Requires JavaScript",
  "inLanguage": "en",
  "isAccessibleForFree": true,
  "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" },
  "publisher": { "@id": "https://rigfloorhq.com/#organization" }
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="ECD Calculator: Equivalent Circulating Density"
  description="Free ECD calculator: annular velocity, annular friction pressure loss, and equivalent circulating density from mud weight, flow rate and annulus geometry."
  extraJsonLd={[webAppLd]}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Calculators', href: '/calculators' }, { label: 'ECD', href: '/calculators/ecd' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/calculators/ecd"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/calculators/ecd" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Calculator</div>
    <h1>ECD <span>Calculator</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Static mud weight only tells you the pressure of a column sitting still. Equivalent Circulating Density adds the annular friction penalty from actually pumping, which is the number the formation feels once circulation starts.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <div class="calc-box">
          <div class="calc-title">🌀 Mud &amp; Flow Data</div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ecd-mw">Mud Weight (ppg)</label>
              <input type="number" id="ecd-mw" value="12.0" step="0.1">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ecd-tvd">True Vertical Depth (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="ecd-tvd" value="10000">
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ecd-q">Flow Rate (gpm)</label>
              <input type="number" id="ecd-q" value="400">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ecd-len">Annulus Length (ft)</label>
              <input type="number" id="ecd-len" value="10000">
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="calc-title" style="margin-top:24px;">⭕ Annulus Geometry</div>
          <div class="input-row">
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ecd-dh">Hole / Casing ID (in)</label>
              <input type="number" id="ecd-dh" value="8.5" step="0.01">
            </div>
            <div class="input-group">
              <label for="ecd-dp">Drill Pipe / Collar OD (in)</label>
              <input type="number" id="ecd-dp" value="5.0" step="0.01">
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="calc-output visible" aria-live="polite" id="ecd-output" style="margin-top:20px;">
            <div class="output-line"><span class="output-label">Annular Velocity</span><span class="output-value" id="out-av">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line"><span class="output-label">Annular Friction Pressure Loss</span><span class="output-value" id="out-apl">—</span></div>
            <div class="output-line headline" style="border-bottom:none;"><span class="output-label">Equivalent Circulating Density</span><span class="output-value" id="out-ecd">—</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="danger-box mt-24" style="max-width:700px;">
          <strong>This is a reference tool, not an operational authority.</strong> Real ECD management decisions belong to the mud engineer and your certified well control supervisor, using measured rheology and your company's approved hydraulics software. Never plan a real drilling window margin from this calculator alone.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The Formulas Used</h2>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Annular Velocity</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>V = (24.5 × Q) ÷ (Dh² − Dp²)</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">Flow rate in gpm, hole/casing ID and pipe OD in inches, velocity in ft/min.</dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Annular Friction Pressure Loss</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>P = (1.4327 × 10⁻⁷ × MW × L × V²) ÷ (Dh − Dp)</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">A field-units estimate of the frictional pressure added by circulating fluid back up the annulus over its full length.</dd>
        </dl>
        <dl class="formula-block">
          <dt class="formula-label">Equivalent Circulating Density</dt>
          <dd class="formula-eq"><code>ECD = MW + (P ÷ 0.052 ÷ TVD)</code></dd>
          <dd class="formula-note">Static mud weight plus the friction pressure converted back into an equivalent mud weight over true vertical depth.</dd>
        </dl>

        <div class="note-box">
          <strong id="worked-example">Worked example:</strong> 12.0 ppg mud, 400 gpm, 10,000 ft TVD and annulus length, through an 8.5 in hole around 5.0 in pipe.<br />
          V = (24.5 × 400) ÷ (8.5² − 5.0²) = 9,800 ÷ 47.25 = <span class="highlight">207 ft/min</span>.<br />
          P = (1.4327×10⁻⁷ × 12.0 × 10,000 × 207²) ÷ (8.5 − 5.0) = <span class="highlight">211 psi</span>.<br />
          ECD = 12.0 + (211 ÷ 0.052 ÷ 10,000) = <span class="highlight">12.41 ppg</span> — 0.41 ppg heavier than static mud weight alone, from friction.
        </div>

        <h3>Where This Fits</h3>
        <p>Static mud weight, the number the <a href="/calculators/hydrostatic">hydrostatic pressure calculator</a> works from, is only the starting point. ECD is the number to check against fracture gradient once pumps are on — the same comparison the <a href="/calculators/mud-weight-window">mud weight window calculator</a> runs between pore and fracture pressure, just against the higher, circulating figure instead of the static one. If a kick has already occurred, the <a href="/calculators/kill-sheet">kill sheet calculator</a> is the next stop, not this one. For the full theory behind the pore pressure/fracture pressure window ECD has to stay inside, see the <a href="/pillars/hydrostatic-pressure">hydrostatic pressure pillar</a>. On a directional or horizontal well, remember measured depth drives velocity and friction along the whole annulus length, but TVD alone still converts that friction back into an equivalent mud weight — the two depths do different jobs in this formula, same as everywhere else on this site.</p>

        <p>This is a single-section estimate treating the annulus as one uniform gap. A real string with drill pipe, heavy weight, and collars in different hole and casing sizes has a different velocity and friction loss in each section, which is why a full hydraulics program run by a mud engineer will not match this number exactly — use this to understand the shape of the problem, not to replace that program.</p>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        {faq.map((item) => (
          <div style="margin-bottom:18px;">
            <h3>{item.q}</h3>
            <p set:html={item.a} />
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
    <NewsletterSignup lang="en" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .output-line.headline .output-value { color: var(--amber); font-size: 18px; }
</style>

<script is:inline src="/scripts/calculators/ecd.js" defer></script>
